{
  "id": "nr___the_noise_rating_curve",
  "title": "NR - the Noise Rating Curve",
  "description": "Determine acceptable indoor environment noise levels for hearing preservation, speech communication and annoyance using Noise Rating curves. Compare measured sound pressure levels at different frequencies to find the appropriate NR rating.",
  "type": "calculator",
  "components": [
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": "## Noise Rating Calculator\n\nEnter the sound pressure levels (in dB) measured at different octave band frequencies to determine the Noise Rating - NR. The NR is determined by finding the highest ratio between measured values and the NR curve values.\n\n### Recommended NR Levels:\n- **NR 25**: Concert halls, broadcasting studios, churches\n- **NR 30**: Private dwellings, hospitals, theaters, cinemas, conference rooms\n- **NR 35**: Libraries, museums, schools, hospitals, executive offices\n- **NR 40**: Offices, restaurants, shops\n- **NR 45**: Department stores, supermarkets, general offices\n- **NR 50**: Typing pools, offices with machines\n- **NR 60**: Light engineering works\n- **NR 70**: Foundries, heavy engineering works"
    },
    {
      "type": "calculator",
      "inputs": [
